    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Essential tremor (G25.0)</content>. Neurology finds the picture typical of essential tremor by the MDS consensus classification: bilateral upper-limb action tremor present for more than three years with head and voice tremor and no other neurological signs, and nothing to suggest Parkinson&apos;s disease or dystonia. Thyroid and metabolic causes are excluded, and she takes no drug that worsens tremor. The hand tremor interferes with daily tasks and her work, so treatment is reasonable. She has no asthma, slow pulse, heart block, or diabetes, so propranolol is suitable. [S]<list><item>Start propranolol extended-release 60 mg each morning, as neurology advises; do not stop it suddenly.</item><item>Report lightheadedness, wheeze, unusual tiredness, or low mood.</item><item>Heart rate and seated and standing blood pressure at the three-month review.</item><item>Occupational therapy for adaptive aids.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Voice tremor due to essential tremor (R49.8)</content>. Mild wavering on sustained vowels that matters for her stage work. [S]<list><item>Speech-language therapy course for voice tremor, starting in two weeks.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Tremor rating: TETRAS performance and activities of daily living subscales</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Performance subscale 22 of 64, unchanged from four weeks ago; daily-living subscale 14 of 48 four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Archimedes spiral drawing and handwriting sample</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Tremor waves in both spirals, right more than left; handwriting legible but shaky</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Examination for signs beyond tremor (rest tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, dystonia, ataxia, cognitive change)</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly or with a change in the tremor</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Neurology finds no rest tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, dystonia, or ataxia, and MoCA was 28 four weeks ago: essential tremor, not essential tremor plus</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Resting heart rate and seated and 3-minute standing blood pressure</td><td>Every visit on a beta-blocker</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline before propranolol: pulse 76; 130/76 mmHg seated and 128/76 mmHg standing</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Beta-blocker safety: wheeze, slow pulse or heart block, fatigue, cold hands, low mood, hypoglycemia awareness</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Before starting: no asthma, wheeze, slow pulse, heart block, or diabetes; PHQ-9 2 four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Primidone safety: sedation, unsteadiness, nausea</td><td>Every visit on primidone</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not on primidone; it is the alternative if propranolol fails or is not tolerated</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Drugs and substances that worsen tremor (beta-agonists, valproate, lithium, antidepressants, excess thyroid hormone, caffeine)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No medicines; one cup of coffee in the morning</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fall query, with Timed Up and Go yearly</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No falls; Timed Up and Go 9 seconds four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood (PHQ-9)</td><td>Before a beta-blocker, after starting it, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 2 four weeks ago, before the beta-blocker</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH and basic chemistry to exclude metabolic causes</td><td>Once at diagnosis; again only with a change in the tremor</td><td>Up to date</td><td>TSH 2.2 and basic chemistry normal four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Movement disorders neurology consultation</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly or with a change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today: diagnosis confirmed clinically and propranolol chosen</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Speech-language therapy for voice tremor</td><td>When voice tremor affects speech or work</td><td>Referred</td><td>Six-week course arranged, starting in two weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Occupational therapy for adaptive aids</td><td>When tremor limits daily tasks</td><td>Referred</td><td>Recommended by neurology today; to be booked</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Botulinum toxin for head or voice tremor</td><td>If head or voice tremor is disabling</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Head tremor does not trouble her and voice tremor is mild; offered later if either becomes disabling</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Referral for focused ultrasound thalamotomy or deep brain stimulation</td><td>If disabling limb tremor persists after adequate trials of propranolol and primidone</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No medicine has been tried yet</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Cognitive screen (MoCA)</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>MoCA 28 of 30 four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>AGS Beers Criteria medication review</td><td>Yearly from age 65</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No medicines on the Beers list; propranolol is not on it</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Advance care planning and goals of care</td><td>At least yearly from 65</td><td>Due</td><td>Planned for the annual wellness visit in the autumn</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Vaccination status (influenza, pneumococcal, zoster, COVID-19, Tdap)</td><td>Yearly, per the CDC schedule</td><td>Due</td><td>Influenza vaccine given last autumn; others at the annual wellness visit</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Less hand tremor with daily tasks on a well-tolerated medicine, with resting heart rate 55 or above and no lightheadedness, by the three-month review. In progress; target 1980-05-05 [S]</item><item>Complete a course of voice therapy and use the strategies on stage. In progress; target 1980-05-05 [S]</item><item>Use adaptive aids for eating, drinking, and writing. In progress; target 1980-10-06 [S]</item><item>Name a health care agent and record her goals of care by the annual wellness visit. In progress; target 1980-10-06 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline update: treatment of essential tremor: report of the Quality Standards Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology (2011); Practice parameter: therapies for essential tremor: report of the Quality Standards Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology (2005); Consensus Statement on the classification of tremors, from the task force on tremor of the International Parkinson and Movement Disorder Society (2018); MDS evidence-based review of treatments for essential tremor (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, general items for older adults (no essential tremor monograph) (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Three-month tremor review with heart rate and standing blood pressure on propranolol. Voice therapy starts in two weeks; occupational therapy to be booked. Next scheduled visit 1980-05-05: three-month tremor review after starting propranolol and voice therapy. [S]</paragraph></text>

